WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:01.734
-In your early --

00:00:01.734 --> 00:00:02.969
-I can hear
the cameramen breathing.

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:04.036
[ Laughter ]

00:00:04.036 --> 00:00:05.605
-In your early days on stage,

00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:07.039
did you ever have
audiences this light?

00:00:07.039 --> 00:00:09.475
-Oh, my Lord.
I did an off-Broadway play

00:00:09.475 --> 00:00:11.510
where, yeah, there were
more people on stage

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:12.779
than in the audience.

00:00:12.779 --> 00:00:15.748
And I remember the director said
to us at one point,

00:00:15.748 --> 00:00:18.417
"You know what I think
the problem is?

00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:20.420
I think the lights
are too bright."

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:21.620
[ Laughter ]

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.591
And I said, "Yes.
That's the problem.

00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:28.828
They can see the play."
[ Laughter ]

00:00:28.828 --> 00:00:32.765
Well, Seth, you're the only one
I get out of my sweatpants for,

00:00:32.765 --> 00:00:34.200
and I mean that
in the nicest way.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.434
-I really appreciate it.

00:00:35.434 --> 00:00:37.603
-Not the mention
the nominal appearance fee.

00:00:37.603 --> 00:00:41.040
-Right, but it's not nothing,
in this day and age.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.909
-I'll tell you.
-There was a real dark spot

00:00:42.909 --> 00:00:45.377
there for Broadway, and then,
last night, a lot of openings.

00:00:45.377 --> 00:00:46.646
-I know.
-Were you out and about?

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:47.747
Did you go see anything?

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:49.516
Did you talk to friends
who are on stage?

00:00:49.516 --> 00:00:51.551
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:53.219
-Who are you talking to,
Kristen Chenoweth?

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:56.055
[ Laughter ]
No, I wasn't "out and about."

00:00:56.055 --> 00:00:57.256
[ Laughter ]

00:00:57.256 --> 00:00:59.692
No.
I don't -- No.

00:00:59.692 --> 00:01:02.929
I'm not going into a theater
with 1,200 people.

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:04.997
Are you nuts?
[ Laughter ]

00:01:04.997 --> 00:01:06.999
Who knows where they've been?

00:01:06.999 --> 00:01:10.203
I wish you all a lot of luck
with that Broadway thing.

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:11.804
[ Laughter ]

00:01:11.804 --> 00:01:13.706
No.
No.

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:17.476
I heard -- yes,
a lot of shows are opening,

00:01:17.476 --> 00:01:21.381
and I hear some of them,
they're selling very well.

00:01:21.381 --> 00:01:23.282
-Good.
-And, look, everyone should be

00:01:23.282 --> 00:01:25.651
cautiously optimistic.

00:01:25.651 --> 00:01:27.420
-It would be nice.
I think those are

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.888
the perfect combination of words

00:01:28.888 --> 00:01:30.055
for how we should be
moving forward.

00:01:30.055 --> 00:01:32.758
-Well, you know,
because we have to be aware

00:01:32.758 --> 00:01:34.927
that there's the delta variant,
now the mu.

00:01:34.927 --> 00:01:36.328
-Yeah.
-The mu, which is not only

00:01:36.328 --> 00:01:39.265
respiratory, but, you know,
there's lactating involved.

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:41.968
[ Laughter ]

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:44.003
Thanks.
Can you run around the room

00:01:44.003 --> 00:01:45.939
and make it sound like a group?

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:49.542
[ Laughter ]

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:51.144
-This, by the way,
is as hot as it's been.

00:01:51.144 --> 00:01:52.712
[ Laughter ]

00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:54.380
We'll send you tapes
of some other guests.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.082
You'll be like,
"Oh, I murdered."

00:01:57.082 --> 00:01:58.951
-Yes.
-You did --

00:01:58.951 --> 00:02:02.288
-Started Wally's comedy career.
-Yeah, and now Wally complains

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:03.756
when he's not in the show
for a week.

00:02:03.756 --> 00:02:04.991
-I know.
-Yeah.

00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:06.993
-Well, it's gone to his head.
-Yeah.

00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:09.095
Well, you know.
You've worked with divas.

00:02:09.095 --> 00:02:11.898
[ Laughter ]
-I sure have.

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:14.166
-You were between a couple,
Marty and Steve, in the clip.

00:02:14.166 --> 00:02:15.735
-Oh, those two.
-Right.

00:02:15.735 --> 00:02:18.338
-Those two
and their shenanigans.

00:02:18.338 --> 00:02:21.274
You know,
someone was asking me --

00:02:21.274 --> 00:02:23.575
They said,
what was the energy like?

00:02:23.575 --> 00:02:27.580
And I said, you know,
they're like two 20-year-olds.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.815
You know --
Okay, maybe 30.

00:02:29.815 --> 00:02:32.718
30-year-olds.
Two tired 30-year-olds.

00:02:32.718 --> 00:02:33.987
[ Laughter ]
All right.

00:02:33.987 --> 00:02:37.156
Two 40-year-olds on vacation.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:37.156 --> 00:02:38.791
Let's put it that way.

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:43.028
No, they are -- you know, I've
known them both a long time.

00:02:43.028 --> 00:02:48.234
And, you know, they're at
that point in their lives where,

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:49.869
you know,
they're bona fide geniuses.

00:02:49.869 --> 00:02:51.537
-Yeah.
-They have nothing to prove.

00:02:51.537 --> 00:02:54.007
They just want to show up
together and have fun

00:02:54.007 --> 00:02:56.776
and do good work
and make millions of dollars.

00:02:56.776 --> 00:02:58.077
[ Laughter ]

00:02:58.077 --> 00:02:59.712
-That's always been a really
important thing for them.

00:02:59.712 --> 00:03:01.914
-And it seems to be working.
No, they're --

00:03:01.914 --> 00:03:05.317
No, they were --
they set the tone

00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:08.154
for the whole set, and you know,
they're so gracious and kind

00:03:08.154 --> 00:03:11.757
and appreciative
of all the guest stars.

00:03:11.757 --> 00:03:13.426
-Yeah, it's a really nice thing
about them.

00:03:13.426 --> 00:03:15.495
And they do --
I've always said about them,

00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:18.397
they put so much work into it,
to make it look effortless.

00:03:18.397 --> 00:03:20.432
-Yes.
Oh, totally!

00:03:20.432 --> 00:03:23.202
Yeah, no, they both --
Especially when they --

00:03:23.202 --> 00:03:24.771
when they do talk shows,

00:03:24.771 --> 00:03:26.205
it's like they're
gonna host the Oscars.

00:03:26.205 --> 00:03:29.976
They prepare.
I myself usually prepare a lot.

00:03:29.976 --> 00:03:31.878
You know,
this was a last-minute booking.

00:03:31.878 --> 00:03:33.345
-It is.
You bailed us out.

00:03:33.345 --> 00:03:35.381
-Who fell out, Lizzo?
[ Laughter ]

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:36.448
-Yeah.
-Lizzo fell out,

00:03:36.448 --> 00:03:38.150
and you thought --
-Lizzo fell out,

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:41.520
and she suggested you.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.656
-"Who's like Lizzo?
Call Nathan lane.

00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:45.491
We know
he's definitely available."

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:46.693
[ Laughter ]

00:03:46.693 --> 00:03:49.929
Does he know the words
to "Truth Hurts"?

00:03:49.929 --> 00:03:50.996
For the kids.

00:03:50.996 --> 00:03:53.132
I do one for you,
one for the kids.

00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:54.600
[ Laughter ]

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:56.903
-I want to pitch you a Broadway
musical that I'm working on,

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:59.071
and I think there's
a really good part for you.

00:03:59.071 --> 00:04:01.040
-"Cicada Cicada."
-"Cicada Cicada."

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:05.144
-Well, the whole town is abuzz.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.144 --> 00:04:07.814
Literally.
With "Cicada Cicada," you know.

00:04:07.814 --> 00:04:09.315
[ Laughter ]

00:04:09.315 --> 00:04:11.083
-You just made the poster,
"Cicada Cicada --

00:04:11.083 --> 00:04:13.453
The whole town is abuzz."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:13.453 --> 00:04:14.820
Look, if you don't get the part,

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:15.954
and I think
you're gonna get it --

00:04:15.954 --> 00:04:17.289
-What do you mean, "if"?
-I'm just saying --

00:04:17.289 --> 00:04:20.159
-What do you mean, if?
-I think you're gonna get it.

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:22.595
But, at the very least,
you have the tagline.

00:04:22.595 --> 00:04:24.964
-I don't have to audition, do I?
-No, I don't think you do.

00:04:24.964 --> 00:04:27.267
-Because I can't audition.
-No, this is --

00:04:27.267 --> 00:04:29.034
We did hear it was offer only.
-Yeah.

00:04:29.034 --> 00:04:30.970
-And as soon as Lizzo said no,
we were like,

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:33.139
"You know who we're gonna get?"
[ Laughter ]

00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:36.108
-Lizzo.
-So, can I just talk through

00:04:36.108 --> 00:04:37.309
the plot real quick?
And you tell me,

00:04:37.309 --> 00:04:38.711
as someone who's
a sort of Broadway pro --

00:04:38.711 --> 00:04:40.680
-Well, from what I hear of
the plot, it has to be quick.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:41.881
[ Laughter ]

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:44.216
-Great.
-Yeah, go ahead.

00:04:44.216 --> 00:04:47.020
-All right, so a Cicada is,
you know, hatched.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:49.255
-Yes.
-Played by Christine Baranski,

00:04:49.255 --> 00:04:52.759
who is a very soft yes.

00:04:52.759 --> 00:04:54.860
[ Laughter ]

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:57.196
-She's an old friend of mine.
She will definitely do it.

00:04:57.196 --> 00:04:58.831
-Okay, great.
-We're working together

00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:01.367
on "The Gilded Age" for HBO.
-Oh, fantastic.

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:03.970
I'm very excited about that.
-She'll definitely do it.

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.872
-Okay. So we got Baranski
is a cicada...

00:05:06.872 --> 00:05:09.608
-A cicada.
-...who loves Broadway,

00:05:09.608 --> 00:05:11.711
born somewhere in Georgia
or South Carolina,

00:05:11.711 --> 00:05:14.413
wherever cicadas are.
-So a gay cicada.

00:05:14.413 --> 00:05:17.850
-Well, yeah, a very gay cicada
comes to New York City.

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:23.188
So excited, but is unaware about
their very short life span.

00:05:23.188 --> 00:05:26.125
This is the tragedy inherent
in the play.

00:05:26.125 --> 00:05:29.494
And it all takes place
during the pandemic.

00:05:29.494 --> 00:05:32.464
And --
-I'm --

00:05:32.464 --> 00:05:34.500
I'm not Bill Nye
the Science Guy.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:35.734
What is their life span?

00:05:35.734 --> 00:05:38.003
-17 days.
-17 days.

00:05:38.003 --> 00:05:39.671
-I've also done
very little research.

00:05:39.671 --> 00:05:41.240
-Oh, wow.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:43.008
Their life span is 17 days?

00:05:43.008 --> 00:05:44.777
-In the play,
it's definitely 17 days.

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:46.045
-Wow.

00:05:46.045 --> 00:05:47.913
-There's a cockroach
in the play.

00:05:47.913 --> 00:05:51.316
-Always. That sounds
like a good part for me.

00:05:51.316 --> 00:05:52.985
-Harvey Fierstein is gonna
play the cockroach.

00:05:52.985 --> 00:05:54.553
-Oh, Jesus, him.
-Sorry.

00:05:54.553 --> 00:05:57.056
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:57.056 --> 00:05:59.525
-So, there's the cockroach,
and the cockroach befriends

00:05:59.525 --> 00:06:01.660
the cicada --
-What's his life span?

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:04.297
[ Laughter ]
-Harvey?

00:06:04.297 --> 00:06:06.966
Well, no, so that's the --
-The cockroach,

00:06:06.966 --> 00:06:08.534
he can live forever.
-Sure.

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:11.003
-All right.
-Anyway, so the cockroach

00:06:11.003 --> 00:06:12.838
realizes his friend the cicada
is gonna pass away

00:06:12.838 --> 00:06:14.940
before Broadway reopens
'cause it takes place

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:17.276
during the pandemic,
and Broadway is closed.

00:06:17.276 --> 00:06:19.512
The cockroach happens to live in
a very famous Broadway --

00:06:19.512 --> 00:06:21.914
-Is the cockroach an old friend
of the cicada's?

00:06:21.914 --> 00:06:24.417
-No, they meet in New York.
She's, like, new off the bus.

00:06:24.417 --> 00:06:25.951
-Only 17 days.
-Yeah.

00:06:25.951 --> 00:06:27.887
-So they meet?
-Yeah, that kind of like

00:06:27.887 --> 00:06:29.155
"cricket in Times Square"
type meeting.

00:06:29.155 --> 00:06:30.189
-Okay.

00:06:30.189 --> 00:06:31.224
-You know what
I'm talking about.

00:06:31.224 --> 00:06:33.659
-Yeah.
-So anyway --

00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:35.294
The cockroach happens to live
in the apartment

00:06:35.294 --> 00:06:37.362
of a very famous Broadway star.
-Yeah.

00:06:37.362 --> 00:06:40.566
-Nathan Lane.
-The cockroach in my apartment?

00:06:40.566 --> 00:06:43.102
-Yes.
-Well, what does that

00:06:43.102 --> 00:06:44.603
say about me personally?
[ Laughter ]

00:06:44.603 --> 00:06:48.441
That I don't keep a clean house?
-You're spinning too much.

00:06:48.441 --> 00:06:51.043
It's not.
The cockroach makes a --

00:06:51.043 --> 00:06:52.845
-That sounded
right out of the shtetl.

00:06:52.845 --> 00:06:54.313
[ Laughter ]

00:06:54.313 --> 00:06:57.183
"You think
I don't keep a clean house?"

00:06:57.183 --> 00:06:59.385
[ Laughter ]

00:06:59.385 --> 00:07:03.623
So, well --
So I have a cockroach.

00:07:03.623 --> 00:07:06.125
-You have a cockroach, and the
cockroach makes a deal with you.

00:07:06.125 --> 00:07:08.694
-Yeah.
-"I will move out of your home."

00:07:08.694 --> 00:07:10.830
-"I'd like to make
a deal with you."

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:12.865
-See? Very good.
You can be the understudy.

00:07:12.865 --> 00:07:14.733
On the nights where Harvey's
sick, you can do both parts.

00:07:14.733 --> 00:07:16.369
-Thanks a lot.
He'd love that.

00:07:16.369 --> 00:07:19.872
[ Laughter ]
-So you -- he convinces you...

00:07:19.872 --> 00:07:22.508
-Yeah.
-...to do a one-man show

00:07:22.508 --> 00:07:25.377
for his friend the cicada
before she dies.

00:07:25.377 --> 00:07:26.812
-Called "Cicada Cicada"?

00:07:26.812 --> 00:07:28.814
-No, that play
is called "Cicada Cicada."

00:07:28.814 --> 00:07:30.549
We can call it whatever we want
in the body of the show.

00:07:30.549 --> 00:07:32.351
-Oh.
-But that's how --

00:07:32.351 --> 00:07:35.154
it would be that you, then,
are the one-man Broadway show.

00:07:35.154 --> 00:07:37.990
-Called "17 Days."
-"17 Days," yeah.

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:40.159
And I think
it'll be really good.

00:07:40.159 --> 00:07:42.828
-But that's it, there's just
three people in this musical?

00:07:42.828 --> 00:07:44.029
-Well, there's other characters.

00:07:44.029 --> 00:07:45.364
Like, a lot of The Mets
are gonna be in it.

00:07:45.364 --> 00:07:49.134
[ Laughter ]

00:07:49.134 --> 00:07:50.936
-Yeah, 'cause what else
are they doing?

00:07:50.936 --> 00:07:52.170
-But there's --
Well, here's the thing.

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:54.706
The Mets are really bad
when the play starts.

00:07:54.706 --> 00:07:56.008
-Well, that comes
as no surprise.

00:07:56.008 --> 00:07:59.445
-With each of the 17 days,
The Mets keep winning.

00:07:59.445 --> 00:08:01.180
-Oh, oh!
-And the cockroach, at the end,

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:04.816
has to decide if he's gonna --
-Is this set in '69?

00:08:04.816 --> 00:08:08.754
-No, it's set in modern times.
Which is why --

00:08:08.754 --> 00:08:11.757
Well, it's kind of a fictional
role, because The Mets do well.

00:08:11.757 --> 00:08:13.726
[ Laughter ]
And then --

00:08:13.726 --> 00:08:15.861
-Well, they won that one --
Wasn't it '69?

00:08:15.861 --> 00:08:18.964
-'86 they won again.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:08:18.964 --> 00:08:22.001
-We're a couple sports guys.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:23.268 --> 00:08:24.603
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:24.603 --> 00:08:28.140
-Anyway, the cockroach --

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:30.242
-I think I hurt myself.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:30.242 --> 00:08:32.044
-The cockroach
has to make a choice --

00:08:32.044 --> 00:08:34.679
"Do I go to this Mets game..."
-Right.

00:08:34.679 --> 00:08:37.483
-"...or do I go to this
one-man -- the chance to see

00:08:37.483 --> 00:08:40.886
my friend the cicada
see Broadway on her --"

00:08:40.886 --> 00:08:43.288
And at the end of your show,
the cockroach gives

00:08:43.288 --> 00:08:45.157
a standing ovation,
but the cicada doesn't.

00:08:45.157 --> 00:08:46.125
You know why?
-Why?

00:08:46.125 --> 00:08:49.562
-Chk! It died.
-Wow.

00:08:49.562 --> 00:08:51.163
-Yeah.
-This has a tragic ending.

00:08:51.163 --> 00:08:53.598
-It's really tragic.
-[ Laughs ] This is --

00:08:53.598 --> 00:08:55.033
-I think everybody thinks
it's gonna be a comedy,

00:08:55.033 --> 00:08:56.435
and that's where we get them
at the end.

00:08:56.435 --> 00:09:00.072
-You know, like, you know,
when you take improv classes,

00:09:00.072 --> 00:09:01.874
you know, "Yes and..."
-Yeah.

00:09:01.874 --> 00:09:05.645
-I think this is a great idea.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:05.645 --> 00:09:09.114
And I just need to speak
to a few people about it.

00:09:09.114 --> 00:09:10.982
You know, Sondheim.

00:09:10.982 --> 00:09:12.217
-Well, we'd love
to get Sondheim.

00:09:12.217 --> 00:09:13.752
-Would you like to get Sondheim?
-Yeah.

00:09:13.752 --> 00:09:16.088
This is going to be
the Stephen, not Gary, right?

00:09:16.088 --> 00:09:18.090
I've been stuck with Gary.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:20.026
If I get stuck with
Gary Sondheim one more time...

00:09:20.026 --> 00:09:24.229
-Gary stinks,
but Steve is really good.

00:09:24.229 --> 00:09:27.599
I would -- I would --
I certainly would sign on

00:09:27.599 --> 00:09:29.669
if you'll have me.
-Yes, great.

00:09:29.669 --> 00:09:32.271
All right, so I got you,
soft yes on Baranski.

00:09:32.271 --> 00:09:34.607
You work on her,
and then Fierstein.

00:09:34.607 --> 00:09:36.976
-Harvey Fierstein.
Have you talked to him?

00:09:36.976 --> 00:09:38.377
-I haven't talked to him yet.
-Yeah.

00:09:38.377 --> 00:09:40.012
-And then, The Mets,
and we're good.

00:09:40.012 --> 00:09:43.015
-"Only Murders in the Building"
is streaming now on Hulu.

00:09:43.015 --> 00:09:44.950
